DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmaalllloocc() function allocates uninitialized memory in kernel address
     space for an object whose size is specified by _s_i_z_e.

     The mmaalllloocc__ddoommaaiinnsseett() variant allocates memory from a specific numa(4)
     domain using the specified domain selection policy.  See domainset(9) for
     some example policies.

     The mmaalllloocc__aalliiggnneedd() and mmaalllloocc__ddoommaaiinnsseett__aalliiggnneedd() variants return allo‐
     cations aligned as specified by _a_l_i_g_n, which must be non-zero, a power of
     two, and less than or equal to the page size.

     Both mmaalllloocc__eexxeecc() and mmaalllloocc__ddoommaaiinnsseett__eexxeecc() can be used to return exe‐
     cutable memory.  Not all platforms enforce a distinction between exe‐
     cutable and non-executable memory.

     The mmaallllooccaarrrraayy() function allocates uninitialized memory in kernel ad‐
     dress space for an array of _n_m_e_m_b entries whose size is specified by
     _s_i_z_e.

     The mmaallllooccaarrrraayy__ddoommaaiinnsseett() variant allocates memory from a specific
     numa(4) domain using the specified domain selection policy.  See
     domainset(9) for some example policies.

     The ffrreeee() function releases memory at address _a_d_d_r that was previously
     allocated by mmaalllloocc() for re-use.  The memory is not zeroed.  If _a_d_d_r is
     NULL, then ffrreeee() does nothing.

     Like ffrreeee(), the zzffrreeee() function releases memory at address _a_d_d_r that
     was previously allocated by mmaalllloocc() for re-use.  However, zzffrreeee() will
     zero the memory before it is released.

     The rreeaalllloocc() function changes the size of the previously allocated mem‐
     ory referenced by _a_d_d_r to _s_i_z_e bytes.  The contents of the memory are un‐
     changed up to the lesser of the new and old sizes.  Note that the re‐
     turned value may differ from _a_d_d_r.  If the requested memory cannot be al‐
     located, NULL is returned and the memory referenced by _a_d_d_r is valid and
     unchanged.  If _a_d_d_r is NULL, the rreeaalllloocc() function behaves identically
     to mmaalllloocc() for the specified size.

     The rreeaallllooccff() function is identical to rreeaalllloocc() except that it will
     free the passed pointer when the requested memory cannot be allocated.

     The mmaalllloocc__uussaabbllee__ssiizzee() function returns the usable size of the alloca‐
     tion pointed to by _a_d_d_r.  The return value may be larger than the size
     that was requested during allocation.

     Unlike its standard C library counterpart (malloc(3)), the kernel version
     takes two more arguments.  The _f_l_a_g_s argument further qualifies
     mmaalllloocc()'s operational characteristics as follows:

     M_ZERO  Causes the allocated memory to be set to all zeros.

     M_NODUMP
             For allocations greater than page size, causes the allocated mem‐
             ory to be excluded from kernel core dumps.

     M_NOWAIT
             Causes mmaalllloocc(), rreeaalllloocc(), and rreeaallllooccff() to return NULL if the
             request cannot be immediately fulfilled due to resource shortage.
             Note that M_NOWAIT is required when running in an interrupt con‐
             text.

     M_WAITOK
             Indicates that it is OK to wait for resources.  If the request
             cannot be immediately fulfilled, the current process is put to
             sleep to wait for resources to be released by other processes.
             The mmaalllloocc(), mmaallllooccaarrrraayy(), rreeaalllloocc(), and rreeaallllooccff() functions
             cannot return NULL if M_WAITOK is specified.  If the multiplica‐
             tion of _n_m_e_m_b and _s_i_z_e would cause an integer overflow, the
             mmaallllooccaarrrraayy() function induces a panic.

     M_USE_RESERVE
             Indicates that the system can use its reserve of memory to sat‐
             isfy the request.  This option should only be used in combination
             with M_NOWAIT when an allocation failure cannot be tolerated by
             the caller without catastrophic effects on the system.

     Exactly one of either M_WAITOK or M_NOWAIT must be specified.

     The _t_y_p_e argument is used to perform statistics on memory usage, and for
     basic sanity checks.  It can be used to identify multiple allocations.
     The statistics can be examined by ‘vmstat -m’.

     A _t_y_p_e is defined using _s_t_r_u_c_t _m_a_l_l_o_c___t_y_p_e via the MMAALLLLOOCC__DDEECCLLAARREE() and
     MMAALLLLOOCC__DDEEFFIINNEE() macros.

           /* sys/something/foo_extern.h */

           MALLOC_DECLARE(M_FOOBUF);

           /* sys/something/foo_main.c */

           MALLOC_DEFINE(M_FOOBUF, "foobuffers", "Buffers to foo data into the ether");

           /* sys/something/foo_subr.c */

           ...
           buf = malloc(sizeof(*buf), M_FOOBUF, M_NOWAIT);


